<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polans (western)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polanie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polans were a West Slavic tribe of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in the basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> river around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>. In the 10th century their Piast rulers, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, united the neighbouring tribes into the Polish state, which took its name from them. They are distinct from the East Slavic Polans of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polans-western.jpg" alt="Polans (western)" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedros.lol · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grody_w_okresie_Mieszka_I.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> derives from that of the Polans, which in turn likely comes from the Polish word for field: the members of a Polan opole drew most of their income from the cultivated field. The chronicler Jan Długosz wrote that Lechites who lived on fields were called Polans by their compatriots in the forests. Zygmunt Gloger, in 1903, linked the name to a growing need for agriculture around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a>.</p><p>The term is not well attested in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its oldest known mention is in the first biography of Saint Adalbert, dated to 998–999, which calls Bolesław I the Brave Bolizlav Polanorum duce in the context of 995. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a>, writing in 1075–1080, placed the Polans beside the Pomeranians on the Oder, bordering the Prussians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and the Rus. Modern historiography disputes the concept of a Polan tribe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The usual account, called the Polan hypothesis, holds that the Polans were a powerful tribe in Greater Poland from the 8th century, led by the House of Piast, who united Poland by conquest in the 10th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, baptised in 966. The historian Przemysław Urbańczyk has said the tribe did not exist, though this claim has been widely rejected. Their foremost gords were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, Grzybowo, Moraczewo, Giecz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, Bnin and Ląd; scholars debate whether Giecz or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> was the original centre.</p><p>The Piast seizure of power probably came late, around the 940s, and was largely peaceful. The 930s and 940s saw an explosion of fort building that Andrzej Nowak called a Polan Big Bang. Archaeology then shows signs of violent conquest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> and the Lublin Land. By the early 990s Mieszko controlled most of Greater Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, much of Silesia and possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Polan culture and society resembled those of the rest of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The traveller Ibrahim ibn Yaqub, writing of his journeys in 962–964, described the realm of Mieszko, whom he called Mashaqqah, as the largest of the Slavic countries. He wrote that Mieszko paid three thousand mailed warriors from taxes collected in market weights, and that dowries were large, so a man with two or three daughters grew rich and one with two sons grew poor.</p><p>Urbańczyk describes early Polan society as egalitarian, a character others call democratic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> was a pagan cult site and a place of assemblies before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> chose it as their capital. Excavations in the late 1990s and early 2000s showed that Ostrów Lednicki and Ląd existed before Piast rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polans (western)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 960 Mieszko I of the Piast dynasty ruled the <b>Polans</b> of Greater Poland from strongholds at Gniezno and Poznan.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-poland">Duchy of Poland</a></li></ul></section>
